## Knowledge Synthesis — Reading with Memory

The wiki is a living knowledge base, not a daily snapshot. Every summary and digest must be written in light of what the wiki already knows.

### What to look up before writing

1. **Read the relevant concept pages** — `wiki/<concept>/<concept-name>.md`. They compress all prior work into one page.
2. **Scan recent digests** — the last 5–7 daily digests. Look for "Worth Watching" bullets or open questions that today's sources address.
3. **Read prior summary pages directly** when the source is in a narrow area you've covered recently.

### Signals to name explicitly

**Confirmation — a pattern is building:**
> "This is the second paper this month (after [title] on [date]) showing X. A pattern is forming."

**Contradiction — a genuine tension exists:**
> "[Paper A] argued X. [Paper B] argues the opposite. Whether these are compatible claims is unresolved."

**Gap-filling — today's paper solves a prior open problem:**
> "The [date] [title] Research Angle asked whether X was possible. [Today's paper] answers: [answer]."

**N-of-a-kind — a pattern has been established:**
> "This is the third paper this week making [claim]. The community has converged on this frame."

The threshold for declaring a pattern: ≥3 sources making the same core claim.

